course: dictionary
lesson: 1194
title: Words that hit hard
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are strong, and they show you how a writer feels. Brutal means very cruel and violent, or very harsh and severe. To find the right meaning, read the words around it. «Sugar cane was grown in brutal conditions, where many people died young.» The words died young show that the conditions were cruel and dangerous, so brutal means cruel here.
explain: Brutal can also describe weather or a hard test. «The winter was brutal, with ice on the roads for weeks.» Nobody is being cruel to the weather, so here brutal means very harsh and severe. Always ask: what word would fit? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: When people say brutal honesty, they mean the truth said directly, even when it hurts. «That maths test was brutal» is informal slang for a very hard test. Words can change with place too: in Ireland, brutal can mean very bad, so context and a dictionary help you check.
fact: Brutal means very cruel and violent.
fact: Brutal can also mean very harsh, severe or unpleasant, as in «a brutal winter».
fact: «Brutal honesty» means the truth said directly, even when it hurts.
fact: In everyday talk, «brutal» can mean really hard, as in «That maths test was brutal»: this is informal slang.
fact: History books use brutal to describe cruel treatment, such as work done in brutal conditions.
exercise: «The winter was brutal, with ice on the roads for weeks.» What does brutal mean here? | Very quiet and calm | *Very harsh and severe | Very funny | Very warm and sunny
exercise: Aisha says her friend's honesty was brutal. What does she mean? | A long story about animals | Very loud singing | A kind secret told quietly | *The truth said directly, even though it hurts
exercise: «Sugar cane was grown in brutal conditions, where many people died young.» Find and copy one word that tells you the conditions were cruel. | young | sugar | *brutal | grown
test: «Enslaved people were worked in brutal conditions.» What does brutal mean? | Very kind and gentle | *Very cruel and violent | Bright and shiny | Quick and fun
test: Ruby says, «That maths test was brutal!» What does she mean? | *It was very hard, which is informal slang | It was written in another language | It was very quiet | It was short and fun
exam: Mia gives brutal honesty about her drawing. What does this mean? | She draws in a very dark colour | She hides the truth to be polite | *She tells the truth directly, even though it hurts | She says the truth directly, even though it hurts
